const tierEntries = tiers.flatMap((tier) => tier.entries);
for (const entry of tierEntries) {
  if (unreleasedMegas.has(entry.name) && !entry.projection) entry.projection = "Unreleased";
}
if (tierEntries.length !== 95 || new Set(tierEntries.map((entry) => entry.name)).size !== 95) {
  throw new Error("Mega tier list must contain all 95 branches exactly once");
}
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Mega Tier List — Super Max + Moves"
  description="Pokemon GO Mega and Primal rankings after Super Max additional Charged Attacks, covering raid damage, Party Power duos, teammate boosts, candy utility, and gyms."
>
  <article class="tool-page mega-tier-page">
    <header class="tool-page-header">
      <p class="tool-kicker">Current ranking · Mega Level 4 · updated August 31, 2026</p>
      <h1>Mega Tier List</h1>
      <p>
        A mechanical Mega and Primal ranking after the Super Max “+” attack update. Party-of-2 raid
        performance is the main combat lens. Two mutually boosted active Megas are compared against
        the best non-Mega pair supported from the back by Primal Kyogre, Primal Groudon, or Mega
        Rayquaza. Every tier follows an explicit combat, candy, full-pool type-leader, or coverage
        rule.
      </p>
    </header>

    <aside class="archive-note" aria-label="Historical tier list">
      <div>
        <strong>Pre-update baseline.</strong> The original visual tier list is preserved separately
        for comparison with the post-“+” ranking.
      </div>
      <a href={originalTierList} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
        View the pre-“+” tier list on TierMaker
      </a>
    </aside>

    <section class="result-panel methodology" aria-labelledby="methodology-heading">
      <h2 id="methodology-heading">How the tiers were judged</h2>
      <p class="evidence-standard">
        <strong>All evidence, no vibes.</strong> Every placement must resolve to a stated tier rule
        and a reproducible calculation; every boss claim must also name the profile. Raw DPS movement
        alone is not utility and does not automatically move a Pokemon between tiers.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Every Mega and Primal attacker is modeled at level 52 with 15/15/15 IVs. Ordinary
          attackers remain level 50.
        </li>
        <li>
          An attached active “+” attack receives the exact Mega Level 4 multiplier of 1.3. Power is
          never rounded in the calculation: Outrage+ uses 185 × 1.3 = 240.5.
        </li>
        <li>
          Party Power timing is modeled for parties of two, three, and four. Party of 2 is the
          binding raid mode; the ordinary raid and Gym modes determine only their stated type floors.
        </li>
        <li>
          In the active-Mega strategy, both Trainers use the same candidate. A Mega does not boost
          itself, so each one receives only the other Trainer's aura. Every Fast and Charged Attack
          is evaluated independently at 1.3× when covered by that aura and 1.1× otherwise, and the
          best moveset is selected after those multipliers apply.
        </li>
        <li>
          The competing background strategy gives both Trainers the highest-DPS repeatable non-Mega
          plus a backline Primal Kyogre, Primal Groudon, or Mega Rayquaza. The audit separately
          reselects the non-Mega moveset under Water/Electric/Bug, Ground/Fire/Grass, and
          Flying/Dragon/Psychic coverage, then binds to the fastest of the three strategies.
        </li>
        <li>
          Both sides are symmetric duos, so the factor of two cancels: one boosted active Mega's DPS
          is compared with one background-supported non-Mega's DPS. This is an active-segment
          benchmark; a full six-slot faint, relobby, and backline-entry simulation can differ.
        </li>
        <li>
          “Outright duo leader” means the highest mutually boosted Mega or Primal for that defense
          profile, listed only if it also beats the binding background strategy. “Catch-aligned duo
          leader” applies the same background check after requiring the candidate's aura to cover the
          type of the Pokemon actually caught.
        </li>
        <li>
          Tier assignment is automatic. A requires at least one result that is both outright and
          catch-aligned. B requires a catch-aligned result but no combined result. C+ requires an
          outright result but no catch-aligned result. The highest applicable result wins.
        </li>
        <li>
          With no qualifying boss result, C requires the fastest neutral Party-of-2 strategy for a
          Charged Attack type included in the candidate's own aura. This comparison includes every
          mutually boosted active Mega or Primal and the best repeatable non-Mega under each of the
          three backline auras; there is no Mega-only leaderboard.
        </li>
        <li>
          C− applies the same full-pool rule to Gym damage, comparing every Mega, Primal, and
          non-Mega attacker without a teammate aura. Normal is excluded because it cannot deal
          super-effective damage, and additional “+” attacks are unavailable in Gym mode.
        </li>
        <li>
          The remaining coverage rules are also exact. D+ is an unduplicated multi-type package. D
          contains exact duplicates when none of those duplicates reaches C−. E applies when an exact
          duplicate reaches C− or higher. F contains monotypes and otherwise-unique packages strictly
          contained by a higher-tier backline aura, such as Scovillain under Primal Groudon.
        </li>
        <li>
          Catch-candy alignment uses the encounter rather than the raid-boss form. Mega raids award
          the ordinary Pokemon, fusion raids award regular Necrozma or Kyurem, and Crowned raids
          award the Hero form. See the <a
            href={officialMegaEncounter}
            target="_blank"
            rel="noreferrer">official Mega encounter rule</a
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          All 95 numeric Mega and Primal branches in the current Game Master are ranked as attackers.
          As targets, ordinary Mega bosses are excluded, while Legendary or Mythical Mega and Primal
          branches remain included as requested. Unreleased branches are explicitly projections.
        </li>
        <li>
          The target sweep contains 188 profiles: every Legendary, Mythical, and Ultra Beast base
          profile in the Game Master, every distinct typed form represented there, and all 18
          Arceus and 18 Silvally types. Unannounced profiles—including regular Zygarde and
          Magearna—are labeled foreseeable assumptions rather than announced raids.
        </li>
        <li>
          The profile sweep is a coverage check, not an equal-weight score. Survival, boss moves,
          damage-derived energy, weather, breakpoints, relobbies, and when the backline booster
          enters battle can change a specific raid simulation.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The official update says additional attacks scale with Mega Level and cannot be used against
        Gym defenders. The ordinary teammate-aura and non-stacking rules are in the <a
          href={officialMegaBoost}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer">Mega battle-bonus help</a
        >. See the <a href={officialMegaUpdate} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"
          >Mega Finale announcement</a
        > for the additional-attack rules.
        Released or announced target labels are cross-checked against <a
          href={officialRaidPool}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer">Road of Legends</a
        >, the <a href={officialMegaFinale} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Mega Finale</a>, and the
        <a href={officialHoopaRaid} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Hoopa Unbound Elite Raid</a>.
        Every other Game Master target is visibly treated as a foreseeable assumption. Within
        each tier, entries are ordered by National Pokedex number rather than strength. Run <code
          >npm run audit:megatier</code
          > to regenerate the target pool, background comparisons, full-pool type leaders,
        mechanical tiers, and per-boss evidence whenever the Game Master changes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="result-panel move-impact" aria-labelledby="move-impact-heading">
      <div class="section-heading-row">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Pending Game Master</p>
          <h2 id="move-impact-heading">Announced “+” attacks</h2>
        </div>
        <span>2 announced</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        Only the two moves not yet present in the current Game Master are listed here. Party-of-2
        gain compares the same level-52 Mega with and without the projected move before a teammate
        aura is applied. The percentage is a combat input, not a tier score.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Pokemon</th>
              <th>Additional attack</th>
              <th>Base power</th>
              <th>Level 4 power</th>
              <th>Party 2 gain</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              plusMoves.map((move) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{move.pokemon}</th>
                  <td>{move.move}</td>
                  <td>{move.basePower}</td>
                  <td>{move.effectivePower}</td>
                  <td>{move.party2Gain}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="projection-note">
        <strong>Announced projection:</strong> The <a
          href={officialMegaSquads}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer">Mega Squads announcement</a
        > gives Fell Stinger+ and Dark Pulse+ base powers of 140 and 150 for September 8. Their full
        combat records are not active in the Game Master yet. The estimates use the ordinary move's
        duration and 100 energy, matching the permanent “+” attacks already in the Game Master, and
        will need rechecking when the records go live. Those permanent moves are calculation inputs,
        so they are not repeated in this table.
      </p>
    </section>

    <nav class="tier-jump" aria-label="Jump to tier">
      {tiers.map((tier) => <a href={`#tier-${tier.slug}`}>{tier.label}</a>)}
    </nav>

    <div class="tier-stack">
      {
        tiers.map((tier) => (
          <section
            id={`tier-${tier.slug}`}
            class="result-panel tier-section"
            data-tier={tier.slug}
            aria-labelledby={`tier-${tier.slug}-heading`}
          >
            <div class="tier-heading">
              <span class="tier-marker" aria-hidden="true">{tier.label}</span>
              <div>
                <h2 id={`tier-${tier.slug}-heading`}>{tier.label} Tier</h2>
                {tier.principle && <p>{tier.principle}</p>}
              </div>
            </div>
            <ul class="tier-members">
              {tier.entries.map((entry) => (
                <li
                  class:list={[
                    "tier-member",
                    entry.rationale && "tier-member-detailed",
                    entry.projection && "tier-member-projected",
                  ]}
                >
                  <div class="member-title">
                    <strong>{entry.name}</strong>
                    {entry.change && <span class="change-badge">{entry.change}</span>}
                    {entry.projection && <span class="projection-badge">{entry.projection}</span>}
                  </div>
                  {entry.rationale && <span class="member-rationale">{entry.rationale}</span>}
                  {entry.evidence && (
                    <dl class="matchup-evidence">
                      {entry.evidence.outrightAligned && (
                        <div>
                          <dt>Outright + catch-aligned duo leader</dt>
                          <dd>{entry.evidence.outrightAligned}</dd>
                        </div>
                      )}
                      {entry.evidence.outright && (
                        <div>
                          <dt>Outright duo leader</dt>
                          <dd>{entry.evidence.outright}</dd>
                        </div>
                      )}
                      {entry.evidence.candyAligned && (
                        <div>
                          <dt>Catch-aligned duo leader</dt>
                          <dd>{entry.evidence.candyAligned}</dd>
                        </div>
                      )}
                    </dl>
                  )}
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </section>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

